3165	  /// for `lag2 = lag1; lag1 = v`, writing lag1's buffer first makes lag2 read the
3166	  /// new lag1 and the loop silently carries `lag2 == lag1` ([#2481]).
3167	  ///
3168	  /// Returns indices into `*copies` in emission order. A copy cycle — the swap
3169	  /// `a, b = b, a`, where each carry's value lives in the other's buffer — has no
3170	  /// valid order, so its members are spilled into scratch buffers first (appended
3171	  /// to `*spills`, which the caller emits ahead of every copy) and their copies
3172	  /// read the spills instead. A spill destination is a fresh allocation nothing
3173	  /// else writes, so spilling removes every outgoing edge of the spilled copy.
3174	  ///
3175	  /// The graph is built once and consumed incrementally: a spill drops only the
3176	  /// spilled copy's own outgoing edges, so the traversal resumes where it stalled
3177	  /// instead of rebuilding anything. Victims come from the residual graph's
3178	  /// strongly connected components, so a node merely downstream of a cycle is
3179	  /// never spilled, and one victim set is enough to leave the residual acyclic —
3180	  /// the traversal stalls at most once.
3181	  ///
3182	  /// Cost is O(k log k + E) in one loop's carry count k: `carry_index` answers each
3183	  /// source with a binary search plus one step per range it actually overlaps, so
3184	  /// k carries on disjoint slots of one allocation cost k lookups rather than k^2
3185	  /// pairwise tests, and every edge is relaxed at most once.
3186	  std::vector<size_t> OrderCarryCopies(std::vector<CarryCopy>* copies, const CarryRangeIndex& carry_index,
3187	                                       std::vector<StmtPtr>* spills) {
